### Relativistic Heavy Ion Collider

The Relativistic Heavy Ion Collider began its operation in the year 2000 and is one of the two collider facilities (other being the Large Hadron Collider at CERN which started in 2010) currently in operation. So far, it is the only spin-polarized proton collider. The RHIC complex is a series of particle accelerators that enable acceleration and collision of various particle species, ranging from polarized protons to heavy nuclei like gold, through a wide range of center-of-mass energies [51][52][53]. Figure 4.4 gives a brief overview of the luminosities and the running periods for the heavy-ion and the polarized \(p+p\) collisions at RHIC [54]. Table 2.1 summarizes the particle species that are collided at RHIC with their respective energies [54].

