The ionization energy loss (\(dE/dx\)) of the charged particles due to interaction with the medium inside TPC is used for particle identification. The value of \(dE/dx\) for the track is extracted from the energy loss measured from the maximum of 45 pad rows. As the length over which the particle energy loss is measured is short and the fluctuations are large, instead of average \(dE/dx\), the most probable energy loss is measured by calculating the truncated mean of 70% of the of the clusters. Figure 2.7 shows a typical plot of measured \(dE/dx\) as a function of track momentum. The solid lines are the prediction from the Bischel function for different particle species [73]. The typical resolution of \(dE/dx\) in Au+Au collisions is \(\sim\) 8%, which makes separation of pions (\(\pi\)) and kaons (\(K\)) upto a momentum of 0.6 GeV/\(c\)

